{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792332669,"nonce":"7T2QVd55RrEJfuTZ+X1InRapjaA=","title":"error_flag: a raising arm that reaches its check through a merge block is not threaded","message":"An inlined accessor that can raise (Array2D.__getitem__ through _idx: IndexError when x or y is out of range, or when the index is past the array) leaves its raising arm and its normal path merging the value they produce, then the check of the flag after it:\n\n    block47: call @py$raise$IndexError(...); br block51\n    block50: ...; br block51\n    block51: %v206 = phi [...block47], [...block50]; br block32\n    block32: %v183 = phi [%v206, block51], ...; load flag; ne null; brcond -\u003e raise, continue\n\nThe pass threads a predecessor that reaches a check with the flag Set; block47 reaches block51, which is no check, and block51's state is Unknown. So every raising call rejoins the hot path: each check stays, and in LLVM every load after the merge is redone, since a call reaches it on one of its edges. scimark SOR's region had 5 flag loads per iteration of its inner loop and 102 loads in all for six G[x, y] reads; SOR's single iteration was 0.65 to 0.69 s on the Mac against PyPy's 0.37.\n\nFix: before threading, a predecessor that reaches a block of phis and a branch to a check with the flag Set skips it, the check's phis taking what the merge's took; only where the merge's phis feed the check's alone. Repeated for nested merges. After it SOR's region has no flag loads and the iteration is 0.40 to 0.42 s.","files":null}]}